{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Problem\n\n`infra/terraform/bedrock-spend-cap/handler.py` prices CloudWatch token counts\nusing a rate table derived daily from Cost Explorer and matched to models by\nstring heuristics. In the twelve days since the cap was deployed (2026-09-12)\nthat layer produced four defects, all one class — matching model identifiers to\nbilling labels by substring and suffix:\n\n1. `_rate_for`/`_match`: candidate `input-token-count` also matches\n   `...-cache-write-input-token-count` and the longest match wins, so input\n   resolves to cache-write. Fires iff the ModelId has no version suffix — live\n   for `claude-sonnet-4-6` and `claude-opus-4-7/4-8/5`.\n2. `_normalise`: `Nova2.0Lite` gives `nova20lite`, `us.amazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0`\n   gives `nova2litev10`; neither contains the other, so a rate already in the\n   table is missed and the model falls to `UNKNOWN_RATE_PER_1K`.\n3. `BEDROCK_SERVICE_VALUES`, the allowlist from 3083-ada3-3a0f-4051, omits five\n   live Bedrock services, so the CE signal sees about a third of real spend.\n4. `MARKETPLACE_TOKEN_SUFFIXES` handles only CamelCase; snake_case\n   (`USE1_input_tokens_standard-Units`) and `_Global` are skipped silently.\n\nDefects 2 and 4 each reached production unalarmed, the first for seven days.\n\n## Approach\n\nDelete the pricing-derivation layer. Price every token at a ceiling rate for its\ndirection, set above the highest per-direction rate observed on this account, so\nthe estimate is an upper bound and the cap cannot fire late. Per UTC day:\n\n    spend = max( sum over regions and the four token metrics of\n                   undimensioned CloudWatch Sum * CEILING[metric],\n                 ce_spend )\n\nThe estimate reads exactly the four undimensioned Sum aggregates per region and\nnever adds per-ModelId series to that total; per-ModelId data is read only for the\nwarn/trip log line, which does not feed the estimate. Today's handler reads only\nper-ModelId series precisely because mixing the two double-counts.\n\nCost Explorer stays in enforcement: it is the only signal covering Bedrock spend\nthat publishes no token metric (Cohere Rerank is live; provisioned throughput and\nbatch are structural), and it is orthogonal to all four defects.\n\n**Release discriminator.** An all-empty CloudWatch read is ambiguous: it is both a\nmetrics-delivery gap and the normal idle/new-UTC-day state the automatic release\ndepends on. Empty alone is therefore never treated as unavailable. The cap may\nrelease on an empty read only when CE for that day is available and zero; if CE is\nnonzero or unavailable, the deny is held. A partial-region failure — one region\nraising while another returns data — holds the deny, because the surviving\nregion's total is a floor, not the account total.\n\n**Accepted limitation.** Ceiling pricing over-estimates for models much cheaper\nthan the priciest — the cheapest here are two orders of magnitude below, and two\nbill nothing for cache-write, so the factor is unbounded. A large embedding or\nsmall-model batch job can trip the cap at a small fraction of intended spend.\nAccepted for simplicity; the remedy if it bites is an exact-match\n`{ModelId: rate}` override defaulting to the ceiling, equality only.\n\n## Figures\n\nMachine-local policy bars spend totals, bill figures and back-test distributions\nfrom the tracker, because the tickets branch auto-pushes to the public GitHub\nmirror. The concrete values are in the operator's local-only note\n`~/Desktop/bedrock-cap-redesign-local-only/design.md`, present on the implementing\nworkstation. They are reproducible without it: each CEILING entry is 1.25x the\nhighest per-token rate for that direction across all Bedrock services in the\ntrailing 60 days of Cost Explorer, and the threshold is the intended daily cap\nscaled by the median over-estimate factor over the same window. AC 4 makes the\ncommitted values checkable in-repo regardless.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The seven pricing functions (`_refresh_rates`, `_rate_for`, `_seed_for`,\n  `_normalise`, `_strip_billing_region`, `_marketplace_rate_key`,\n  `_rate_key_for_ce_group`) and six constants (`SEED_RATES`, `RATE_OVERRIDES`,\n  `BEDROCK_SERVICE_VALUES`, `MARKETPLACE_TOKEN_SUFFIXES`, `UNKNOWN_RATE_PER_1K`,\n  `MARKETPLACE_TOKENS_PER_RATE_UNIT`) are absent. Checked: a test asserting no\n  such name survives.\n- [ ] The `TOKEN_METRICS` direction multipliers are removed. Checked: same test.\n- [ ] `_metered_spend` issues exactly four undimensioned `GetMetricData` queries\n  per region. Checked: a test asserting the stub recorded four per region.\n- [ ] `_metered_spend` calls no `list_metrics` on the enforcement path. Checked:\n  a test asserting zero `list_metrics` calls during a tick.\n- [ ] A `CEILING` mapping gives one rate per token metric, each at least the\n  highest for that direction in the fixture. Checked: a test comparing against\n  rates derived from the fixture.\n- [ ] Enforcement compares `max(ceiling_estimate, ce_spend)`. Checked: a test\n  driving each arm larger in turn.\n- [ ] `_ce_spend_today` classifies by predicate (service equals `Amazon Bedrock`\n  or ends with the Bedrock Edition suffix), not an allowlist. Checked: a test\n  proving it matches every Bedrock service in the fixture, including the five\n  omitted.\n- [ ] On an all-empty CloudWatch read the cap releases only when CE that day is\n  available and zero. Checked: two tests — CE zero releases, CE nonzero holds.\n- [ ] On an all-empty read with CE unavailable the deny is held. Checked: a test\n  stubbing a CE exception.\n- [ ] A partial-region failure holds the deny. Checked: a test raising in one\n  region while the other returns data below threshold.\n- [ ] Any exception from a metered or CE read fails closed: no release. Checked:\n  a test raising from each client in turn.\n- [ ] `infra/terraform/bedrock_spend_cap.tf` carries the threshold and warning\n  values from the design note. Checked: `terraform plan`.\n- [ ] The orphaned `SEED_RATES`, `RATE_OVERRIDES`, `UNKNOWN_RATE_PER_1K` and\n  `RATE_WINDOW_DAYS` environment entries are deleted, with\n  `variable \"bedrock_cap_seed_rates\"`, `variable \"bedrock_cap_unknown_rate_per_1k\"`\n  and the `bedrock_cap_seed_rates` / `bedrock_cap_rate_overrides` locals. Checked:\n  `terraform plan` shows exactly those deletions plus the threshold and warning\n  changes.\n- [ ] No unused Terraform declarations remain. Checked: `terraform validate`.\n- [ ] `state[\"rates\"]`, `state[\"rates_day\"]`, `metered_estimated`, `MeteredRate`\n  and the `\" (estimated)\"` rendering are removed. Checked: a test asserting the\n  persisted state carries no rate table.\n- [ ] A daily guard compares the estimate for a settled day against CE and raises\n  an SNS warning when the estimate is below actual. Checked: a test driving a\n  violation.\n- [ ] That guard cannot attach or detach the policy. Checked: same test asserting\n  no IAM call.\n- [ ] Per-model token counts are logged on warn and trip paths only, no prices, no\n  matching. Checked: a test asserting the line on trip, absent on a normal tick.\n- [ ] CE and `list_metrics` fixtures are committed with structure recorded from\n  this account but synthetic dollar amounts and usage quantities, since the repo\n  is public. Checked: a test asserting no fixture amount matches billing.\n- [ ] The CE fixture retains CamelCase, snake_case, `_Global` and a non-token\n  usage type. Checked: a test asserting each shape is present.\n- [ ] Every ModelId in the `list_metrics` fixture is priced by the ceiling with no\n  fallback path. Checked: a property test over the fixture.\n- [ ] The README's \"How spend is measured\" no longer describes bill-derived rates,\n  the seed fallback order or `UNKNOWN_RATE_PER_1K`. Checked: a test asserting\n  those strings are absent.\n- [ ] The README states the accepted over-estimate, the effective-cap band\n  qualitatively, and that the figure is an upper bound. Checked: reading it.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `env PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\" pytest tests/infra/test_bedrock_spend_cap_handler.py -q`\n  proves every criterion naming a test.\n- `env PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\" make lint`, `make typecheck`, then `make verify`\n  before push per AGENTS.md.\n- `python3 scripts/terraform_prod.py plan --out <scratch>/plan.tfplan` must show\n  exactly the env deltas above before any apply.\n\n## Rollback\n\nImmediate mitigation: set `DRY_RUN=true`, which stops the function attaching or\ndetaching while leaving it observable. Full back-out: revert the Gerrit change and\nre-run the mediated plan and apply. Terraform packages the handler by content hash,\nso the revert restores prior code and env exactly, and the deleted state keys are\nrebuilt on the next tick because the old code refreshes them when absent.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nSSM write concurrency and the `signal_unavailable` deadlock: no occurrences in\nfourteen days of logs."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1790288201961692097,"uuid":"181616a5-6ee7-4656-8397-2fa42adc1041"}